 rows 2–9 and columns 4–6, so the dot lies to the right, within the rectangle's rows.\n\n1. **Bar.** A 3-cell bar is drawn perpendicular to the direction of travel, in the column just outside the rectangle (column 7, rows 2–4). It is centred on the dot's row.\n2. **Line.** A straight line runs along the dot's row, from that column up to the cell just before the dot (row 3, columns 7–11).\n3. **Ring.** The dot becomes a hollow plus: its four orthogonal neighbours are coloured and the dot cell itself goes back to background. The changed-cell list confirms (3,12) changes 1→3.\n\nI checked the same pattern by hand against the other connectors:\n\n- **Pair 1, colour 4, right side:** dot (12,13), bar at column 11, line along row 12.\n- **Pair 1, colour 4, left side:** dot (15,3), bar at column 7 rows 14–16, line along row 15 columns 4–7.\n- **Pair 2, vertical case:** dot (1,7) sits above the rectangle (rows 4–5). The bar is row 3 columns 6–8, the line is column 7 rows 2–3, and the ring is around (1,7). The dot below works the same way: bar at row 6, line down column 5, ring around (9,5).\n- **Pair 3:** dots above and below the 8-rectangle. The changed-cell list matches this pattern, for example the long column 8 from row 11 to 16 ending in a ring at row 17.\n\n## The rule\n\nFor each rectangle (the large component), find the same-colour single dots.\n\n- If the dot's row is inside the rectangle's row span, the connector is horizontal. Otherwise it is vertical.\n- Draw the 3-cell bar adjacent to the rectangle's facing edge.\n- Draw the line from the bar up to the cell before the dot.\n- Draw the four ring cells around the dot, then clear the dot to background.\n\n## Applying it to the test\n\n- **Colour 2** (rectangle rows 2–8, columns 10–13): a dot to the right at (3,23) and one to the left at (5,4). Both get horizontal connectors.\n- **Colour 1** (rectangle rows 11–17, columns 15–17): dots at (13,5) on the left and (16,26) on the right. Both get horizontal connectors.\n- **Colour 3** (rectangle rows 23–25, columns 4–13): a dot above at (19,7). It gets a vertical connector with its bar at row 22.\n\nThe program emits one stage per completed connector.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    rects = [o for o in objs if o[\"size\"] > 1]\n    dots = [o for o in objs if o[\"size\"] == 1]\n\n    def put(y, x, c):\n        if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W:\n            out[y, x] = c\n\n    for r in rects:\n        c = r[\"colour\"]\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = r[\"box\"]\n        for d in dots:\n            if d[\"colour\"] != c:\n                continue\n            dy, dx = d[\"cells\"][0]\n            if y0 <= dy <= y1:  # horizontal connector\n                if dx > x1:\n                    s, step = x1 + 1, 1\n                else:\n                    s, step = x0 - 1, -1\n                for yy in (dy - 1, dy, dy + 1):\n                    put(yy, s, c)\n                x = s\n                while x != dx:\n                    put(dy, x, c)\n                    x += step\n            else:  # vertical connector\n                if dy > y1:\n                    s, step = y1 + 1, 1\n                else:\n                    s, step = y0 - 1, -1\n                for xx in (dx - 1, dx, dx + 1):\n                    put(s, xx, c)\n                y = s\n                while y != dy:\n                    put(y, dx, c)\n                    y += step\n            for ny, nx in ((dy - 1, dx), (dy + 1, dx), (dy, dx - 1), (dy, dx + 1)):\n                put(ny, nx, c)\n            out[dy, dx] = bg\n            emit(out, f\"Connected colour-{c} rectangle to dot at ({dy},{dx}): bar at the rectangle edge, line to the dot, dot turned into a hollow plus ring.\")\n    emit(out, \"All dots connected to their rectangles.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"paths":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The output leaves every rectangle where it was and builds one connector toward each dot."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Here is the connector for the 1-coloured dot at (3,12) in pair 1."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Connected colour-1 rectangle to dot at (3,12): bar at the rectangle edge, line to the dot, dot turned into a hollow plus ring."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"8a9df23561c5b2509bb86d4ecf43071ed454a6bc9c8e8f2dfd7f2e8042691c06","task_sha256":"f8430cf3063da237646a7e7b2c1d830b25ecdbf014a7e36afd10a26c10e22767","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
